			"description": "Excessive Data Exposure\r\n\r\nA web frontend built against this endpoint would render a comment's text, author, and map position, and silently drop any other field the API happens to send back. The bug is in the response payload itself, not in any particular client: read the raw JSON (with curl, or a browser's Network tab if a frontend were calling this) and one field is present that no UI was ever meant to show.\r\n\r\nNote on this folder's data model: `routes/api.php` only defines `POST api3/user`, `POST api3/user/login`, `GET api3/comment` and `POST api3/comment` for API3. There is no \"get user by id\" route here. A comment record carries a `username` field, not a numeric user id, and logging in as that username (`POST api3/user/login`) only ever returns your own username on success -- it does not unlock any further data. The exposure is self-contained in the comments response below; it does not require chaining through a separate user-lookup endpoint. (API1 and API5 do have id-based user lookups, if that is the pattern you are after.)\r\n\r\n### Read the raw API response\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -v https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/comment | python3 -m json.tool\r\n```\r\nLook at every key in the returned object, not just the ones a UI would display. `postid`, `commenttext`, `latitude`, `longitude` and `username` are what a comment widget would show; `deviceid` is backend bookkeeping that leaked into the public response.\r\n\r\n### The rest of the flow, for completeness\r\n```bash\r\n# Register a user\r\ncurl -s -X POST https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/user \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d '{\"username\":\"u1\",\"name\":\"U\",\"password\":\"p1\"}'\r\n\r\n# Log in as that user (returns only your own username on success)\r\ncurl -s -X POST https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/user/login \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d '{\"username\":\"u1\",\"password\":\"p1\"}'\r\n\r\n# Post a comment, exactly as a client app would\r\ncurl -s -X POST https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/comment \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d '{\"postid\":\"1\",\"deviceid\":\"whatever-a-client-app-would-send\",\"latitude\":\"0\",\"longitude\":\"0\",\"commenttext\":\"hi\",\"username\":\"u1\"}'\r\n\r\n# Re-read the comment list: your own deviceid value comes back in the response, same as the seeded one\r\ncurl -v https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/comment\r\n```",
